      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hartford%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Local tradition suggests Hartford was named after a deer crossing, or hart ford, on the Rough River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hartford_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Fort Hartford was established before 1790 as one of the first permanent settlements in the Green River Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hartford_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hartford was formally incorporated by the Kentucky General Assembly in 1808 after serving as a frontier station.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hartford_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town became a key stop on the Louisville and Nashville Railroad network in 1909.",
